#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256726 :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=4,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/83/835b4206e04d064485d02635cfd4e2ad39a934b1d2b38296cc6463a265f5d931.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8e0a10d162d30be895fa4ce49e3944e4e1a3ee9f79aa2fafe70c0edd2c0d61.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256728 :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1,$ which gives the vertical hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the pair of lines $y=\\pm x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2,$ which gives the horizontal hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the right half-plane $x>0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the left half-plane $x<0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/03/037f46d3fdd190749c2aefe51222475fdd337a7b3ce53e6809048e3491a31a47.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256730 :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7844777c0bb540ba337756f45c4d71a8a8e9f9c84372e01542892801efecce3.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/98/98f444851e2df505d4242d1f8e4be0d76f78babd5d9633d9ab0ec84c245fe364.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is three-quarters of a circle of radius $r=2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{3}{4}(2\\pi r)=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3\\pi +2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256731 :question/problem "![](images/68/68b3f78c29c9d7776efc2a78672ae53fe2e806d7ca6c3d663de8a16c03342132.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/27/27c19b56a24315f1105539afc441a33957dddf606b3410b86121e7cb8ed16973.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5+2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256733 :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf87c4707c0f4a8ab1b5157a13e8c7785da915833c5f9d896a2747af27f70e59.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/ff/ff52b8e83a9cc9867b25ea3c24ed0f988a27dec73f9e0ab2dfda28ddabb3238a.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}=2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256734 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+1},\\,3t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-k\n$$ is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{2k}{3}\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256735 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,2\\sqrt{t}⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 2 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u) & =⟨4u,\\,2\\sqrt{2u}⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2k\n$$ is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256744 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3t\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-3}{\\frac{2}{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{3}-3t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{3}-3(1)}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256752 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi t)-2t,\\,y={e}^{2t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=2{e}^{2\\times (1/2)}=2e\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256770 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x={t}^{3}-8,\\,y={t}^{4}+5t\n$$\nfrom $t=-2$ to $t=3$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-8)=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}+5t)=4{t}^{3}+5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(4{t}^{3}+5)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+16{t}^{6}+40{t}^{3}+25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{16{t}^{6}+9{t}^{4}+40{t}^{3}+25}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256771 :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a58808ee9b20e201d1ea8a31683f742e02f739127640de2fea87ce4757e7901.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=3{t}^{2},\\,y=5t\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(6t)^{2}+(5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256772 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=3{t}^{3}+5,\\,y={t}^{2}-5t\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=2$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}+5)=9{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-5t)=2t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{(9{t}^{2})^{2}+(2t-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{81{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}-20t+25}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259485 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y-{e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{sin}y). \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259486 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}yz,\\hspace{0.167em}xyz,2⟩.$ Find $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}yz+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259487 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nFind $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x+3y-4z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((2x+3y-4z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((2x+3y-4z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x+3y-4z)+6(2x+3y-4z)-8(2x+3y-4z) \\\\  & =2(2x+3y-4z) \\\\  & =4x+6y-8z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259490 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x-y)^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y+z)^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+z)^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-2,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-y)^{2}+3(y+z)^{2}+3(x+z)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-2,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}(-2,2,2) & =3((-2)-(2))^{2}+3((2)+(2))^{2}+3((-2)+(2))^{2} \\\\  & =3(-4)^{2}+3(4)^{2}+3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259500 :question/problem "![](images/b3/b315be9ba524ab538388fdaad31161c280d7d6e5ab71261345063ac6ffe819d5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $∇\\cdot F>0$ is true, whereas statements $∇\\cdot F=0$ and $∇\\cdot F<0$ are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors increase as we move vertically and horizontally away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∇\\cdot F>0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259501 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t[(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})] \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-1$ and $y=t+1$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =1+(2t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}(t+1) \\\\  & =2t-\\operatorname{cos}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}(t+1))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}(t+1))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259503 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t[(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})] \\\\  & =(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t(5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(5t-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=5t-3$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-3)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2(5t-3)+(1+2t) \\\\  & =12t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\cdot \\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259517 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$ along the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t[(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}] \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2$ and $y=2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2+\\sqrt{2(2t)} \\\\  & =2+2\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t+\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259518 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{0}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{0}) \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =t(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={e}^{3(-t)+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}[{e}^{t}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259523 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ traversed along the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+t[(-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+t(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(5-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-4,y=2t+1,$ and $z=5-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-2t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{(2t+1)+(5-2t)}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{t-2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259524 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+t[(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(t+4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+2,$ $y=t-2,$ and $z=t+4$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(2t+2){e}^{(t-2)(t+4)} \\\\  & =(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+2t-8$, $\\text{d}u=(2t+2)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\int }_{-8}^{-5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{-8}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}({e}^{-5}-{e}^{-8}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{8}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551 :question/problem "![](images/bf/bff60466ca8abba4fd849c046c02af3a4811279774975fc981cafad91d734ed5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259552 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259553 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=-{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259554 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,\\,\\,y=3t+2,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-3$ | ${\\color{blue}a}$ | $1$ | $0$ | ${\\color{blue}c}$ |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | ${\\color{blue}b}$ | $5$ | $8$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $x=1-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=1-(-1)^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $y=3t+2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=3(0)+2=2.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=1-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=1-(2)^{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+2+(-3)=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259555 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{3},\\,-3\\le t\\le 1,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-12$ | $-8$ | ${\\color{blue}a}$ | ${\\color{blue}b}$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $27$ | $8$ | $1$ | $0$ | ${\\color{blue}c}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $x=4t.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=4(-1)=-4.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=4t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=4(0)=0.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=1$ into $y=-{t}^{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=-(1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+0+(-1)=-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259556 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t+9},\\,\\,\\,y=t+1,\\,t\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{t+9}\\ge 3$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Consequently, $x=2\\sqrt{t+9}\\ge 6.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $x\\ge 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259557 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3t-7,\\,\\,\\,y=1-t,\\,t\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $x,$ note that $x=3t-7\\ge -4$ for all $t\\ge 1.$ \n\nConsequently, the range of $x$ is $x\\ge -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259558 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-4t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,t\\le 0,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $x,$ note that $x=1-4t\\ge 1$ for all $t\\le 0.$ \n\nConsequently, the range of $x$ is $x\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259559 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\,\\,y=1-t,\\,t>0,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $y?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $y,$ note that $y=1-t<1$ for all $t>0.$ \n\nConsequently, the range of $y$ is $y<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259560 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3t+2,\\,\\,\\,y=4\\sqrt{t}-1,\\,t\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $y?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $y,$ note that $4\\sqrt{t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Consequently, $y=4\\sqrt{t}-1\\ge -1.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $y\\ge -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259561 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+3 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3\\le 1+3 \\\\ 2 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $2\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1\\le 1-1 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2\\le y\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $2\\le x\\le 4$ and $-2\\le y\\le 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259562 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=1-t,\\,-3\\le t\\le 3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=1-t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/e8/e8cfffa1c9aaba887d90a9251a0c67402343b5a47dbb31cd85c6e648ba800e4e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259563 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t-1,\\,y={t}^{2}-5,\\,-3\\le t\\le 3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t-1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y={t}^{2}-5.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/a9/a9d9be86049e1422d9c5c7e4af24992959637c3771503b13f75865840076149b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259564 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=2-{t}^{2},\\,y=t+1,\\,-1\\le t\\le 3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-7$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-7$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/e0/e06724560817f82e97c4606754a704cef47832ed89ab458dc3072622ce71d158.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259565 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t},\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 4\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\sqrt{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/89/898075d0f74bdfcbab28e50ee327770400f6f58d82728cd8bf55cc75e852d161.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259573 :question/problem "Which of the following regions is *not* connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/10/1067a6abc2249b31f3786a3f277a2db10d8f32cbacf971a550a0473243684da7.png)\n\n \n\nNote that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcaaf15d709d6396155bcdc9e8976bb037c9f341fc0b8863687958dbb0f40ff.png)"}]}
